[516.669971, "o", "\r\nThis file is small and mostly careful, but it leaks in exactly the places the review brief warns about: a rule enforced at several doors and missing at one more. Finding 1 is the clearest — `NullSession` painstakingly re-points seven mutating methods at `_fail`, but `dict.__ior__` (which werkzeug 3.1's `UpdateDictMixin` re-implements with update tracking) is not among them, so `sess |= {...}` on a secret-key-less app silently accepts and then discards the write instead of raising the loud configuration error the class exists to produce; the LocalProxy forwards `|=`, so this is reachable from ordinary view code. Finding 2 is the more damaging one: `open_session` catches `BadSignature`, but itsdangerous' `BadPayload` is a sibling of `BadSignature` under `BadData`, not a subclass — a cookie with a *valid* signature whose payload the current serializer cannot load (serializer swap, Flask version skew, another service sharing `SECRET_KEY` and the `cookie-session` salt) escapes the handler and 500s every request from that client until they clear cookies, which is precisely the situation the empty-session fallback was written to avoid. Finding 3 is a smaller contract break: `get_cookie_path` is annotated `-> str` and its docstring promises a `/` fallback, but it returns `None` when `APPLICATION_ROOT` is falsy, and werkzeug then omits `Path` entirely. Findings 4 and 5 are read-only observations I did not execute. I did not find anything wrong with the signing-key ordering, the fallback-key handling, the Vary logic, or the modification tracking of the ordinary `SecureCookieSession`.\r\n\r\nfindings: 0 reproduced, 5 code-read, 0 hypothesis\r\n"]
